-- Use check constraints

CREATE TABLE customers (
    customer_id NUMBER,
    customer_name STRING,
    status STRING,
    
    CONSTRAINT valid_status
        CHECK (status IN ('ACTIVE', 'INACTIVE', 'PENDING'))
);

-- Now, if someone tries to insert an invalid value:

INSERT INTO customers
VALUES (101, 'John', 'UNKNOWN');
-- The insert fails because the value violates the CHECK constraint.

Why is this useful?
✅ Enforce data quality at the database layer
✅ Prevent invalid values from entering your tables
✅ Reduce reliance on application-side validation
✅ Make business rules visible as part of the table definition
✅ Apply the same rule consistently across different data pipelines

-- You can also define constraints directly on a column:
CREATE TABLE orders (
    order_id NUMBER,
    order_amount NUMBER CHECK (order_amount >= 0),
    order_date DATE CHECK (order_date <= CURRENT_DATE())
);

Tip: Think of CHECK constraints as a safety net at the table level. Your ETL pipeline can still validate data, 
but the database becomes the final line of defence against invalid values.
